============================================================================
LEGACY.configs — every documented pre/part/post configuration that TrinityX
distributes (roles, daemon plugins, and the mkdocs docs)
============================================================================

Purpose
-------
This is the catalogue of legacy Luna install-hook configurations we ship, so we
can test `install_mode=legacy` against each of them. Under `legacy` the node
runs the original bash **prescript / partscript / postscript** plus a full image
unpack; the new lpart-* Go binaries are NOT invoked. These three hooks ARE what
`legacy` executes, so this file is the test surface for legacy behaviour.

The three hooks (from the daemon plugin docstrings)
---------------------------------------------------
  prescript   runs early, before /sysroot is mounted (set up config / disks)
  partscript  runs before /sysroot is mounted; it is responsible for CREATING
              and MOUNTING the root filesystem at /sysroot (tmpfs or a disk)
  postscript  runs after the image is laid down, before the OS pivot; tailoring,
              /etc/fstab, bootloader install, hostname, etc.

Everything the scripts touch on the target lives under /sysroot (the running
installer is a tmpfs; anything outside /sysroot is lost at pivot).

How to set them on a node or group (the "how to use")
-----------------------------------------------------
  Inline file content (base64 under the hood):
    luna node  change <node>  -qpre  <file>  -qpart <file>  -qpost <file>
    luna group change <group> -qpre  <file>  -qpart <file>  -qpost <file>
  Interactive editor:
    luna node change <node> -pre        (also -part / -post)
  Apply a shipped plugin's pre+part+post in one shot:
    luna node change <node> --scripts <plugin>      # default | diskfull | raid1 | nodhcp
  At group creation (what the group-create role does):
    luna group add --osimage <image> -qpart /tmp/part.rd -qpost /tmp/post.rd <group>

Quick index
-----------
  ROLES (github-trinityX/site/roles)
    1. Diskless tmpfs — RedHat            luna/group-create/files/redhat/{part,post}.rd
    2. Diskless tmpfs — Ubuntu            luna/group-create/files/ubuntu/{part,post}.rd
    3. Diskless tmpfs — openSUSE          luna/group-create/files/opensuse/{part,post}.rd
    4. Cloud single-disk                  cloud/luna/files/{part,post}.rd
  DAEMON PLUGINS (luna2-daemon: daemon/plugins/boot/scripts/)
    5. default plugin (empty baseline)    default.py    (--scripts default)
    6. diskfull plugin                    diskfull.py   (--scripts diskfull)
    7. raid1 plugin                       raid1.py      (--scripts raid1)
    8. nodhcp plugin (single-disk)        nodhcp.py     (--scripts nodhcp)
  MKDOCS (clustervision/trinityx-docs: trinityX/docs/)
    9. Diskfull how-to (live)             howtos/disk-full-node.md
   10. Diskfull recipe (orphan dup)       recipes/disk-full-node.md
   11. Default node-config example        admin/luna.md  (the node show table)
   12. Tailoring / example postscript     admin/node.md
   13. Plugin architecture + list         admin/luna-plugins.md


============================================================================
ROLES — the .rd hook files distributed in github-trinityX/site/roles
============================================================================
These are the default scripts the `luna/group-create` Ansible role drops into
/tmp and assigns to the compute group at provisioning time:
  group-create/tasks/main.yml ->
    luna group add --osimage {{luna_image_name}} -qpart /tmp/part.rd -qpost /tmp/post.rd {{luna_group_name}}
NOTE: the role assigns only part + post (no prescript). The distro is chosen by
the controller OS family (redhat | ubuntu | opensuse).

--- 1. Diskless tmpfs — RedHat ----------------------------------------------
Where:  site/roles/luna/group-create/files/redhat/part.rd  and  .../post.rd
What:   The default. Mounts a RAM (tmpfs) root at /sysroot; the image is
        unpacked into RAM and the node runs entirely from memory. Local disks
        are left untouched (free for scratch). RedHat uses NUMA-interleaved
        tmpfs so memory is spread across all NUMA nodes.
  part.rd:  mount -o mpol=interleave -t tmpfs tmpfs /sysroot
  post.rd:  echo 'tmpfs / tmpfs mpol=interleave 0 0' >> /sysroot/etc/fstab
Use:    Default for a RedHat-family compute group; nothing to do. To set by hand:
        luna group change <group> -qpart redhat/part.rd -qpost redhat/post.rd

--- 2. Diskless tmpfs — Ubuntu ----------------------------------------------
Where:  site/roles/luna/group-create/files/ubuntu/part.rd  and  .../post.rd
What:   Same diskless tmpfs idea, written for the Ubuntu/initramfs-tools boot
        environment, which exposes the root mountpoint as $rootmnt rather than a
        literal /sysroot.
  part.rd:  ls "$rootmnt" || mkdir "$rootmnt"
            mount -t tmpfs tmpfs "$rootmnt"
  post.rd:  echo 'tmpfs / tmpfs defaults 0 0' >> /$rootmnt/etc/fstab
Use:    Default for an Ubuntu-family compute group.

--- 3. Diskless tmpfs — openSUSE --------------------------------------------
Where:  site/roles/luna/group-create/files/opensuse/part.rd  and  .../post.rd
What:   The plainest diskless tmpfs root (no NUMA option, literal /sysroot).
  part.rd:  mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /sysroot
  post.rd:  echo 'tmpfs / tmpfs defaults 0 0' >> /sysroot/etc/fstab
Use:    Default for an openSUSE-family compute group.

--- 4. Cloud single-disk ----------------------------------------------------
Where:  site/roles/cloud/luna/files/part.rd  and  .../post.rd
What:   The cloud variant — boots from a LOCAL DISK instead of tmpfs (cloud
        instances have a disk and no PXE-friendly RAM-root assumption).
  part.rd: auto-detects /dev/sda or /dev/nvme0n1; reuses an existing >=4.5 GB
           "root" partition or (re)creates one with parted using the free tail
           of the disk (failsafe paths included); mkfs.ext4; mount at /sysroot;
           records the device in /tmp/disk.dat.
  post.rd: writes the ext4 / line to /sysroot/etc/fstab (from /tmp/disk.dat, or
           re-detects), and disables NetworkManager DHCP hostname override
           (hostname-mode=none) so the cloud DHCP cannot rename the node.
Use:    Applied by the cloud/luna role on cloud deployments; or by hand:
        luna group change <group> -qpart part.rd -qpost post.rd


============================================================================
DAEMON PLUGINS — shipped script generators (luna2-daemon)
============================================================================
Source:    luna2-daemon: daemon/plugins/boot/scripts/<name>.py
Installed:  /trinity/local/python/.../site-packages/daemon/plugins/boot/scripts/
Each plugin is a Python class exposing prescript / partscript / postscript
strings. Apply all three to a node/group with:
    luna node change <node> --scripts <name>
The plugins are designed to be tuned via an override file the prescript writes
to /tmp (edit it, or pre-seed it, then re-run), so the same plugin serves many
disk shapes without editing the scripts.

--- 5. default plugin (default.py) ------------------------------------------
What:   The baseline plugin: prescript, partscript AND postscript are all EMPTY.
        Applying it clears a node/group back to "no hook scripts". The shipped
        diskless-tmpfs DEFAULT does NOT come from here — it comes from the
        group-create role's part.rd/post.rd (configs 1-3); default.py is the
        empty fallback the plugin loader uses when nothing more specific matches.
Use:    luna node change node001 --scripts default     # reset scripts to empty

--- 6. diskfull plugin (diskfull.py) ----------------------------------------
What:   "Default Install for pre, part and post" — a local single-disk install.
  prescript:  writes /tmp/my-local-disk.sh with the tunables, then prints them:
                MY_LOCAL_DISK_NAME=/dev/sda     # target disk (or /dev/disk/by-uuid/...)
                MY_LOCAL_DISK_SECTORS=          # optional size check
                PARTITION_MY_DISK=yes
                FORMAT_MY_DISK=yes
                MAKE_BOOT=yes                   # install grub/shim for standalone boot
              (if the file already exists it is kept — the override mechanism)
  partscript: sources /tmp/my-local-disk.sh, resolves by-uuid -> kernel name,
              partitions/formats the disk per the tunables, mounts it at /sysroot.
  postscript: finalises the install + bootloader before pivot.
Use:    luna node change node001 --scripts diskfull
        # then (optional) edit the disk via -pre or by pre-seeding /tmp/my-local-disk.sh
        luna node change node001 -pre node001     # tweak the override interactively

--- 7. raid1 plugin (raid1.py) ----------------------------------------------
What:   A software-RAID1 (mirror) root across two local disks.
  partscript: parted lays out each disk: EFI (fat32 1g) + boot (ext4 1-2g) +
              swap (linux-swap 2-4g) + root (ext4 4g-100%, raid flag on);
              mkfs.fat the ESP, mkfs.ext4 boot; then
                mdadm --create /dev/md0 --metadata 1.2 --bitmap=internal --force
                      --assume-clean --level=mirror --raid-devices=2 <d1p4> <d2p4>
              mkfs.ext4 /dev/md0; mounts the mirror at /sysroot.
  postscript: bind-mounts proc/dev/sys, writes mdadm.conf ("AUTO -all" + the
              scanned ARRAY line — note this matches the v1.4 partitioner's own
              auto-assembly guard), runs dracut --add=mdraid --add-drivers=raid1,
              installs grub.
Use:    luna node change node001 --scripts raid1

--- 8. nodhcp plugin (nodhcp.py) --------------------------------------------
What:   A fixed single-disk (/dev/sda) diskful install. Empty prescript.
  partscript: parted /dev/sda -> gpt + EFI(fat32 1g) + boot(ext4 1-2g) +
              swap(linux-swap 2-4g) + root(ext4 4g-100%); waits for the device
              nodes; mkswap+swaplabel sda3; mkfs.fat sda1, mkfs.ext4 sda2 + sda4;
              mounts sda4 -> /sysroot, sda2 -> /sysroot/boot, sda1 -> /boot/efi.
  postscript: restores the 95luna dracut module-setup if stashed, bind-mounts
              proc/dev/sys, and installs a `luna-postinstall.service` systemd
              unit into the image (runs post-install on first boot). The "nodhcp"
              name reflects that this variant manages networking statically rather
              than via DHCP.
Use:    luna node change node001 --scripts nodhcp
        # needs the install_items dracut tools (config 9c) like any disk layout.


============================================================================
MKDOCS — documented configurations in clustervision/trinityx-docs
============================================================================
Repo: clustervision/trinityx-docs  (site under trinityX/, nav in trinityX/mkdocs.yml)

--- 9. Diskfull provisioning how-to (LIVE) ----------------------------------
Where:  trinityX/docs/howtos/disk-full-node.md   (nav: "Diskfull provisioning")
What:   The canonical how-to. Contains, in order:
        a) Use the included diskfull plugin:  luna node change --scripts diskfull
           then  luna node change -pre node001   (edit the disk override)
        b) Use the included raid1 plugin:     luna node change --scripts raid1
        c) install_items: a dracut config snippet to bake mkfs/mdadm/parted/etc.
           into the install initramfs (REQUIRED for any non-diskless layout —
           the diskless default needs none):
             install_items+=" /usr/sbin/mkswap /usr/sbin/mkfs.vfat /usr/sbin/mkfs.fat
               /usr/sbin/mdadm /usr/sbin/fdisk ... "
        d) Default diskless booting: the tmpfs part/post (same as configs 1-3):
             partscript:  mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /sysroot
             postscript:  echo 'tmpfs / tmpfs defaults 0 0' >> /sysroot/etc/fstab
        e) Manual diskfull, by hand:
           prescript (Step 1): parted /dev/sda -> gpt + EFI(fat32 1-512m) +
             boot(ext2 512m-1g) + root(ext4 1g-100%); mkfs.fat/ext2/ext4.
           postscript (Step 2): bind proc/dev/sys, write /sysroot/etc/fstab
             (/dev/sda3 /, sda2 /boot, sda1 /boot/efi), remove the stale Shim
             NVRAM entry, efibootmgr --create a fresh Shim1, grub2-mkconfig.
             (Distro path varies: note the literal 'rocky' in /EFI/rocky/...)
Use:    follow the page; the plugin sections are one-liners, the manual section
        is a copy-paste starting point to adapt per site.

--- 10. Diskfull recipe (ORPHAN duplicate) -----------------------------------
Where:  trinityX/docs/recipes/disk-full-node.md   (NOT in mkdocs.yml nav)
What:   An older, shorter (193 vs 270 line) duplicate of #7 with the same
        diskless tmpfs + disk-full scriptlets.
Note:   Stale/orphaned — not published. Fold into the live howtos/ page (or
        delete) when doing docs cleanup.

--- 11. Default node-config example (the node show table) --------------------
Where:  trinityX/docs/admin/luna.md  (the "luna node show" example table)
What:   Documents the default values a freshly-created node carries, including:
          prescript   <empty> (default)
          partscript  mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /sysroot
          postscript  echo 'tmpfs / tmpfs defaults 0 0' >> /sysroot/etc/fstab
        i.e. confirms the shipped default is diskless tmpfs with no prescript.
Use:    reference for "what does a default node look like".

--- 12. Tailoring / example postscript --------------------------------------
Where:  trinityX/docs/admin/node.md  ("Tailoring scripts" / "Example postscript")
What:   Explains postscripts (valid for Luna v1 AND v2) and the /sysroot rule
        (everything must be prefixed with /sysroot; outside it is the throwaway
        installer tmpfs). Example postscript:
          sed -i "s/HOSTNAME/`hostname -s`/g" /sysroot/etc/myapp.yml
          echo "myhostname = `hostname -f`" >> /sysroot/etc/postfix/main.cf
        Also documents node-level override of group scripts (test a single node).
Use:    pattern for per-node tailoring postscripts.

--- 13. Plugin architecture + full plugin list ------------------------------
Where:  trinityX/docs/admin/luna-plugins.md
What:   Documents the daemon plugin system and lists every shipped plugin,
        including the boot/scripts ones used here:
          boot/scripts/default.py   boot/scripts/diskfull.py
          boot/scripts/nodhcp.py    boot/scripts/raid1.py
        Key philosophy: plugins resolve by OS-distro / group / node override —
        the loader picks the first match, e.g. redhat.py > redhat8.py >
        default.py. So a site can ship its own boot/scripts/<distro>.py to change
        the default pre/part/post without editing anything.
Use:    reference for which --scripts plugins exist and how overrides resolve.


============================================================================
Notes for legacy testing
============================================================================
- The shipped DEFAULT everywhere is diskless tmpfs with NO prescript: partscript
  mounts tmpfs at /sysroot, postscript writes the tmpfs fstab line. This is the
  primary legacy-tmpfs case to validate (configs 1-3 / 11).
- Any disk layout (configs 4, 6, 7, 8, 9e) additionally needs the install_items
  dracut tools in the install initramfs (config 9c); diskless needs none.
- The plugins 5-8 are the "by --scripts" path; the .rd files (1-4) and the manual
  doc steps (9e) are the "by -qpart/-qpost" path. Both end up as the node's
  prescript/partscript/postscript that `install_mode=legacy` runs verbatim.
- Cross-check: the raid1 plugin already writes "AUTO -all" to mdadm.conf — the
  same udev-auto-assembly guard the v1.4 Go partitioner now applies itself.


============================================================================
Deltas on the clustervision development branch (checked 2026-06-07)
============================================================================
Compared the canonical clustervision repo (gitlab clustervision/trinityx-combined,
branch `development`) against `main`. (GitHub clustervision/trinityX has only
`main`, no development.)

- The legacy hook SCRIPTS are UNCHANGED on development: all 8 .rd files
  (redhat/ubuntu/opensuse part.rd+post.rd, cloud part.rd+post.rd) are byte-for-
  byte identical to what is catalogued above. No new .rd files, no renames. The
  diskless-tmpfs and cloud-disk configs themselves did not change.
- What DID change is the ASSIGNMENT plumbing in the roles' tasks:
  * luna/group-create/tasks/main.yml: copies part.rd/post.rd to
    `{{ trix_tmpdir }}` instead of a hardcoded /tmp, and the create command is now
      luna group add --osimage <img> -qpart {{trix_tmpdir}}/part.rd \
        -qpost {{trix_tmpdir}}/post.rd --setupbmc no <group>
    plus an optional follow-up that assigns `default-bmcsetup` on the `ipmi`
    network when both exist.
  * cloud/luna/tasks/main.yml: same /tmp -> {{ trix_tmpdir }} change for the
    cloud part/post copy + group add.
  * luna/osimage-create/tasks/main.yml: kerneloptions tokens gained a leading
    underscore (net.ifnames=0 -> _net.ifnames=0) — cosmetic Luna marker, not a
    hook change.
  * trinity/image-create/vars/{x64,aa64}/image-ubuntu.yaml: `mdadm` ADDED to the
    Ubuntu image package list — relevant for legacy RAID/disk installs on Ubuntu
    (mdadm now present in the Ubuntu osimage).
  * trinity/luna2 (the daemon role): version/template pins changed; since the
    daemon ships the diskfull/raid1/nodhcp/default --scripts plugins, a daemon
    version bump could carry updated plugin bodies (the plugin source is in
    luna2-daemon, not in these roles).
- Net: nothing changes the legacy pre/part/post CONTENT to test; only where the
  role stages the files ({{trix_tmpdir}}) and the added --setupbmc no / Ubuntu
  mdadm package. Test surface above stands.


############################################################################
============================================================================
TEST JOURNAL — legacy pre/part/post validation (started 2026-06-07)
============================================================================
############################################################################

This is the running journal for the legacy-config test campaign. Goal: run each
documented legacy config, find what breaks, FIX it, and end with a comprehensive
"required fixes" doc + the overview table at the bottom.

============================ EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ==============================
*** RE-BASELINED 2026-06-07 (PM) against the REAL source of truth. ***

SOURCE-OF-TRUTH CORRECTION (important — see [[feedback-daemon-source-of-truth]]).
The original F1-F11 below were derived against the WRONG sources: the deployed
runtime daemon (site-packages), the alex/luna2-daemon fork, and prior staged local
patches. The canonical source is `clustervision/luna2-daemon:development`
(GIT-FLOW-006 + REPOS.md). Re-checked against development @ cfbf7c4 (cloned at
/root/git/clustervision-luna2-daemon); several findings were already fixed there.

KEY REFRAME — there is NO lpart in clustervision/luna2-daemon. development (and
main, development-2.0, main-2.0 — no lpart branch) is the CLASSIC daemon: no
install_mode lever, no templ_install_lpart.cfg (404), no shim. So the entire lpart
integration — the F10 install_mode lever, the templ_install.cfg/_lpart rename, the
shim-skip guard, F11's stale regex — is LOCAL-ONLY to this controller + the alex
fork. On stock development, legacy IS the normal classic install path (no TUI by
nature). F10/F11 are therefore NOT upstream defects; they are local lpart-cluster
coexistence concerns.

PER-FINDING STATUS vs development (supersedes the [A]/[B] tags below):
  * F1  raid1 NOT disk-fault-tolerant -> STILL A BUG in development (only partly
        mitigated). development raid1.py RAIDs ONLY root (md0 = p4) and *copies*
        the bootloader to a second ESP (/boot/efi2 on sdb1), but:
          - /boot (kernel + initramfs, p2) is on **sda2 ONLY** -- not mirrored,
            sdb2 left empty; and
          - the two ESP copies are NOT kept in sync (drift on any grub update).
        So losing sda: firmware -> sdb1 ESP -> shim -> grub loads, then grub can't
        find the kernel (/boot was on sda) -> boot fails.
        HW-CONFIRMED 2026-06-07 (EMPIRICAL, operator pulled sda + rebooted):
        node001 landed at the **EFI shell = bricked**. The earlier "both ESPs have
        a bootloader" check was necessary but NOT sufficient -- it only proves the
        bootloader stage, not the kernel/root stage. (Lesson: a degraded boot must
        be proven by a real disk-pull + cold reboot, never by inspection or by a
        post-install "node is up" -- which here reached the OS via the
        PXE->Luna->local-disk chainload and masked the standalone-disk boot.
        See [[regression-firmware-disk-boot-gap]].)
        PROPER FIX = the lpart approach: RAID the WHOLE boot chain -- ESP (md
        metadata 1.0 so firmware still reads it as FAT) + /boot + root -- so every
        stage is mirrored AND kept in sync. lpart was dd-failover-tested and boots
        degraded; development raid1 fails the same test.
        legacy-lint F1 check corrected: greps for a real /boot-mirror marker
        (absent in development) -> reports BUG, not the earlier wrong efi2=FIXED.
  * F3  FAT16 ESP -> FIXED in diskfull + raid1 (mkfs.fat -F 32); REAL only in nodhcp.
  * F4  parted `name 1"..."` typo -> FIXED in raid1 (has the space); REAL in
        diskfull + nodhcp (cosmetic).
  * F5  boot flag on p2 -> development has `set 1 esp on` + `set 2 boot on`; the
        dev diskfull HW boot SUCCEEDED (Shim1/Boot0000 NVRAM entry added), so the
        prior "firmware hang" was the STALE plugin, not development. Treat F5 as
        cosmetic/unconfirmed pending a deliberate re-check.
  * F6  zero stale md superblocks -> FIXED in development raid1 (mdadm --stop +
        the /dev/md/* loop before --create).
  * F8  NVRAM -> development diskfull explicitly adds the Shim1 (Boot0000) entry
        and reorders BootOrder. Improved vs the old finding.
  * F2 (doc example), F7 (nodhcp device-name fstab), F9 (cloud device-name fstab)
        -> STILL REAL on development.
  * F10/F11 -> LOCAL-ONLY (lpart coexistence), NOT upstream. Kept for this
        controller's lpart-cluster legacy path only.

WHAT IS DEPLOYED + VALIDATED (this controller, 2026-06-07 PM):
  * Deployed development's diskfull.py + raid1.py + nodhcp.py and development's
    templ_install.cfg AS the legacy template, re-applying ONLY the lpart-coexistence
    shim-skip (now matches tx-node-installer|lpart-node-installer|lpart-phase). The
    lpart path (lever + templ_install_lpart.cfg) is UNTOUCHED. Backups at
    /root/luna2-v1.4-patches/legacy/backup-20260607-devsync.
  * HW: legacy DISKFULL (development plugin + template) installs END-TO-END and
    boots (sda4 ext4 root, UUID fstab, Shim1/Boot0000 NVRAM entry, status=success).

WHAT REMAINS:
  * HW-confirm development RAID1 (esp. F1 dual-ESP: pull sdb degraded-OK, pull sda
    should NOT brick now), nodhcp, and the tmpfs path. (Note: a legacy/stateful
    node PXE-REINSTALLS on reboot, so dd-failover must be done in a boot-local mode
    to observe degraded/brick LOCAL boot — see the 2026-06-07 PM journal entry.)
  * Upstream (clustervision/luna2-daemon:development, human MR): nodhcp F3/F7,
    diskfull+nodhcp F4, doc F2, cloud F9. Update legacy-lint's F1 check to
    development's dual-ESP approach.

(The original investigation F1-F11 is retained below as the evidence trail; read
this summary for current truth.)
============================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. How legacy actually runs (architecture — read first)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
`install_mode=legacy` is DAEMON-ONLY. The new Go installer refuses it outright:
    cmd/lpart-storage-prepare/decide.go:21
      "install_mode=legacy is daemon-only and must not reach the client"
So under legacy the node does NOT run the lpart-* binaries. The daemon hands the
node the old prescript/partscript/postscript (from the role .rd files or the
`--scripts <plugin>`), and the 95luna dracut network installer (luna2-start.sh)
executes them verbatim in the install initramfs. The lpart-* tools are bypassed.

Consequence for THIS campaign: we are running the OLD bash scripts inside the
CURRENT node image / initramfs / daemon. Every failure is therefore one of:

  CLASS A — new-environment regression.
            The old script worked once but breaks because the CURRENT image,
            initramfs, daemon, tooling, or path moved under it. Fix = restore
            the missing capability in the new env (or adapt the staging), NOT
            the legacy script's logic. Examples to watch:
              * the installer initramfs no longer writes `AUTO -all` to
                mdadm.conf in legacy mode (only lpart-storage-prepare does, and it
                doesn't run here) -> udev incremental assembly can re-grab md
                members mid-partition (the exact race we killed for the new
                installer).
              * a dracut `install_items` tool the old script needs (mkfs.fat,
                mdadm, parted, swaplabel...) dropped from the current compute
                image.
              * the 95luna module path the scripts `rm -rf` moved.

  CLASS B — old-example bug.
            A defect intrinsic to the legacy script/doc that was always wrong
            (or wrong-for-UEFI / not-re-entrant / not-HA). Fix = fix the legacy
            script/plugin/doc itself.

Each finding below is tagged [A] or [B] with a severity (BLOCKER / robustness /
cosmetic) and a proposed fix. The hardware runs confirm or revise the static
calls.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Static analysis (done 2026-06-07, source-level, pre-hardware)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sources read in full: diskfull.py, raid1.py, nodhcp.py, default.py (luna2-daemon
boot/scripts), the 8 role .rd files, and howtos/disk-full-node.md (live + the
commented "inspiration" scriptlets).

F1  [B, BLOCKER for sda-loss]  raid1 plugin mirrors ONLY root, not ESP/boot.
    raid1.py partscript: `set 4 raid on` only; ESP(p1)+boot(p2) are plain and
    mounted from disk1 alone (mount ${DISK1}1 /boot/efi, ${DISK1}2 /boot). md0 is
    the root mirror (p4+p4). So losing DISK1 (sda) leaves a healthy root mirror
    but NO ESP/boot anywhere -> node is unbootable. Losing DISK2 (sdb) is fine
    (ESP/boot live on the survivor sda). HA is therefore asymmetric and broken
    for the sda leg. The new installer fixed exactly this with BootMirrorDevice
    (mirrored os-efi/os-boot md at metadata 1.0). PREDICTS the dd test result:
    pull sdb -> degraded boot OK; pull sda -> bricked. Fix: mirror p1+p2 as
    metadata-1.0 md arrays (md-efi, md-boot) like the doc inspiration example /
    the new installer, write both ESPs, fstab the md.

F2  [B, would-not-run]  doc "inspiration" RAID1 example has a device-path bug.
    howtos/disk-full-node.md commented block: DISK1=/dev/sda then
    `mdadm ... /dev/${DISK1}p1` expands to /dev/dev/sdap1. Wrong on two counts
    (double /dev, and `p1` suffix is for nvme not sata). The example also loops
    `parted /dev/$DISK ...` with DISK already = /dev/sda -> /dev//dev/sda. It is
    commented out (not rendered) so it never runs, but it is the example a user
    copies. Fix: correct to ${DISK1}1 etc, or delete the stale inspiration block.
    NOTE: this commented example is the ONLY raid example that mirrors ESP+boot
    (md0/md1/md2) — i.e. its INTENT is more correct than the shipped plugin (F1),
    it just doesn't execute.

F3  [B, robustness]  FAT16 ESP on every disk plugin. diskfull/raid1/nodhcp all
    `mkfs.fat -F 16` the ESP even though parted labels it fat32. UEFI wants FAT32
    on a >512MB ESP; FAT16 is tolerated by many firmwares but not all (and is
    inconsistent with the parted type). New installer uses vfat/FAT32. Fix:
    `mkfs.fat -F 32`.

F4  [B, cosmetic/non-fatal]  parted name typo. diskfull + nodhcp:
    `parted ... -s 'name 1"EFI System Partition"'` — missing space, parted reads
    the number token as `1"EFI` and errors out (no `set -e`, so it continues; the
    partition just goes unnamed). raid1 has it correct (`name 1 "EFI ..."`). Fix:
    add the space.

F5  [B, cosmetic]  boot flag on the wrong partition. diskfull + nodhcp do
    `set 2 boot on` (flag on /boot, p2) instead of the ESP (p1). On GPT/UEFI the
    "boot" flag marks the ESP. raid1 correctly does `set 1 boot on`. Harmless on
    most UEFI but wrong. Fix: `set 1 boot on`.

F6  [B, re-entrancy]  raid1 plugin never zeroes stale md superblocks before
    `mdadm --create`. Re-running it on disks that already hold an md superblock
    can make mdadm prompt/refuse, or udev can re-assemble a stale array from the
    survivors first (Class-A interaction: no `AUTO -all` in the legacy initramfs).
    This is precisely the re-entrancy failure mode we hardened the new installer
    against (defensive --zero-superblock + wipefs + AUTO -all). Fix: zero + wipefs
    each member before create; write `AUTO -all` early.

F7  [B, robustness]  nodhcp uses a device-name fstab (not UUID).
    CORRECTION (2026-06-07, via legacy-lint against the INSTALLED daemon, not a
    stale backup): the CURRENT nodhcp.py (159 lines, the version the daemon
    actually serves) DOES install a bootloader — efibootmgr Shim1 + grub2-mkconfig
    with DISTRO auto-detect, identical to diskfull's MAKE_BOOT (postscript lines
    ~142-152). An older backup copy lacked it; the shipped one does not. So
    nodhcp is "diskfull pinned to /dev/sda + a first-boot dracut re-run service".
    The remaining real defect: its fstab is hardcoded `/dev/sda4 / ext4` etc.
    (NOT blkid UUID), so it breaks if the kernel enumerates the disk differently
    (added controller, NVMe, etc.). It also carries F3/F4/F5 like the others.
    Fix: switch fstab to UUIDs (as diskfull does).

F8  [B, info]  diskfull NVRAM method vs the new installer. diskfull MAKE_BOOT:
    deletes any existing Shim1, `efibootmgr --disk <d> --part 1 --create --label
    Shim1 --loader /EFI/<distro>/shimx64.efi`, grub2-mkconfig. It does NOT create
    the removable-media fallback EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI, does NOT set BootNext/Order,
    and does NOT clear the firmware-recreated <distro>/fbx64 NVRAM entry. The new
    installer (lpart-bootloader-finalise) adds BOOTX64.EFI fallback + degraded-safe
    grub.cfg + handles the fbx64 CSV. So legacy diskfull is more fragile on
    firmwares that drop/re-order NVRAM (and on VMware, per the §3.4b flakiness in
    the new-installer journal). To analyse empirically in the diskfull hardware run.

F9  [B, robustness, cloud-scoped]  cloud part/post.rd: single ext4 root only.
    No ESP, no /boot, no bootloader install — makes one ext4 "root" partition,
    mkfs.ext4, mounts /sysroot, fstabs it by DEVICE NAME (not UUID). Boots only
    where the cloud platform supplies the boot chain. Uses a `parted
    ---pretend-input-tty | fix` hack to auto-answer GPT-repair prompts (fragile).
    By-design for cloud images; document as "not standalone-bootable on bare
    metal", and the device-name fstab is a fragility note (Class B, cloud niche).

CLASS-A resolution (checked on the controller's /trinity/images/compute-image,
2026-06-07):
A1  RESOLVED — NO baked `AUTO -all` mdadm.conf in the compute image, and
    lpart-storage-prepare (the only writer) does not run in legacy. => the legacy
    install initramfs has NO udev-assembly guard. This CONFIRMS F6 is a real
    exposure: a re-run raid1 plugin can lose the md-create race to udev. (The
    raid1 plugin's own postscript writes AUTO -all into the INSTALLED system's
    mdadm.conf, but that is post-pivot — too late to protect the install-time
    create.) Severity of F6 stands: re-entrancy BLOCKER on second run.
A2  RESOLVED — install_items DOES bake the disk tools:
      mkswap swaplabel mkfs.vfat mkfs.fat mkdosfs mdadm fdisk parted ... (and
      mkfs.ext4 + the binaries are present in the image rootfs). efibootmgr and
      grub2-mkconfig run via `chroot /sysroot` from the installed OS, so they do
      not need to be in install_items. => missing-tool Class-A risk LOW.
A3  RESOLVED — 95luna is at /lib/dracut/modules.d/95luna in the image; the
      diskfull/nodhcp `rm -rf /sysroot/lib/dracut/modules.d/95luna/` path is
      correct. => path-moved Class-A risk NONE.

Net Class-A surface: small. The one live Class-A interaction is A1 (no AUTO -all)
which amplifies the Class-B re-entrancy bug F6. Everything else the current image
still provides.

F10 [A, BLOCKER — legacy is DEAD on the current client]  the lpart thin-shim
    install.sh front-runs lpart-node-installer in every phase, and the part phase
    hard-refuses install_mode=legacy, so NO legacy customscript ever runs.
    CONFIRMED ON HARDWARE (node001, 2026-06-07, diskfull run — see §2a):
      * The daemon, for install_mode=legacy + scripts=diskfull, served the NEW
        lpart-shim install.sh and STAGED the legacy plugin scripts at
        /lunatmp/scripts/diskfull-{pre,part,post}.sh.
      * install.sh runs each phase as: (1) a "thin shim" that execs
        `lpart-node-installer --phase <p>`, then (2) `customscript <p>` which runs
        the staged legacy diskfull-<p>.sh — BUT only if the shim returned 0.
      * Phase pre: lpart pre = prepare-runtime (no storage) -> exit 0 -> diskfull-pre
        ran (we saw "DISKFULL script: my-local-disk override found").
      * Phase part: lpart part -> lpart-storage-prepare -> sees install_mode=legacy ->
          "lpart-storage-prepare: install_mode=legacy is daemon-only and must not
           reach the client"  -> exit status 1
        (decide.go:21). install.sh then prints "Part script exited with exit code
        1. Stop install." and ABORTS -> `customscript part` is never reached ->
        diskfull-part.sh NEVER EXECUTES (grep of the whole install log: no
        "DISKFULL script: creating/formatting/mounting"). 6 retry loops, all
        identical (lpart-node-installer-part.log).
    ROOT CAUSE: a contract break between daemon and client. decide.go says legacy
    "must not reach the client", i.e. the daemon was supposed to (a) never send
    install_mode=legacy to a lpart-shim node, or (b) serve the OLD install.sh for
    legacy (no lpart shims; just unpack + customscript). Neither happened: the daemon
    sent legacy AND served the lpart-shim install.sh. The client correctly refuses;
    the daemon's legacy routing is missing.
    IMPACT: ALL 8 legacy hook configs (diskfull/raid1/nodhcp + the 5 .rd/tmpfs
    cases via customscript) are untestable & unusable as-is on a cluster running
    the current luna2-client — they all die at this same point, before their own
    partscript. This supersedes the per-script findings F1-F9 as the thing to fix
    FIRST: until F10 is fixed, F1-F9 can only be checked statically (legacy-lint).
    Additional secondary conflicts the legacy path would hit even past part (must
    also be handled by the fix): lpart pre drops /lunatmp/skip_unpack (the legacy
    path needs the image unpack), and lpart post = lpart-bootloader-finalise would
    collide with the plugin's own grub/shim postscript.
    REQUIRED FIX (see §4): primarily daemon-side (serve a legacy install.sh, or
    reject legacy at luna-CLI on lpart-client clusters); optionally a clearer
    client-side guard. Tracked for staging, not pushed (clustervision repo).
    SECONDARY OBSERVATION (good news): in THIS install initramfs udev did NOT
    auto-assemble the prior md arrays (mdstat "unused devices: <none>"), so the
    A1/F6 race did not fire on this boot — though sda1/2/3 still carry
    linux_raid_member superblocks (parted never got to repartition them).

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Hardware runs (node001)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2a. diskfull legacy install (2026-06-07) -> FIRST RUN BLOCKED by F10 (install
    fails at partscript "exit code 1" because the lpart-shim part phase refuses
    legacy; diskfull-part.sh never executed). This DIAGNOSED F10.
    --- F10 FIX BUILT + DEPLOYED (2026-06-07) ---
    Implemented the daemon-side fix (staged at /root/luna2-v1.4-patches/legacy,
    deployed live on this controller, see §4 P0 + that README). Naming convention:
    the ORIGINAL name templ_install.cfg is reserved for legacy/original behaviour;
    the v1.4 lpart pipeline is renamed to templ_install_lpart.cfg.
      * daemon/templates/templ_install.cfg (now the LEGACY/original template) —
        restores real unpack_imagefile + download_image, and runs pre/part/post via
        customscript (skipping the lpart-shim FIELD via `grep -q lpart-node-installer`).
        No lpart pipeline, no TUI.
      * daemon/templates/templ_install_lpart.cfg — the v1.4 lpart-pipeline template,
        byte-for-byte the file that used to be templ_install.cfg.
      * daemon/base/boot.py install() lever — default (no-method) path routes by
        install_mode: legacy -> templ_install.cfg; everything else ->
        templ_install_lpart.cfg (mirrors kickstart's per-method template). boot.py
        compiles; daemon restarted clean.
    VERIFIED both renders: GET /boot/install/node001 returns templ_install_lpart.cfg
    when install_mode=auto, and the LEGACY templ_install.cfg when
    install_mode=legacy — real "Un-packing imagefile", legacy shim-guards,
    download_torrent/http injected, customscript wired to diskfull, flow
    prescript->partscript->download_image->unpack_imagefile->postscript, no
    lpart-osimage no-op stub.
    --- SECOND RUN (post-fix) : SUCCESS (2026-06-07) ---
    With the legacy template (templ_install.cfg) deployed, the legacy diskfull ran
    END-TO-END and the node BOOTED the disk. node001 layout (was xfs/LVM under
    auto; now the diskfull scheme):
        sda1 vfat  /boot/efi (953M)   sda2 ext4 /boot (953M)
        sda3 swap  (1.9G)             sda4 ext4 /     (21.3G)
    findmnt / = /dev/sda4 ext4; UUID fstab; luna status="Luna installer: success".
    => F10 FIX VALIDATED: the diskfull plugin's own pre/part/post executed (was
    impossible pre-fix). The fix is correct + sufficient for the --scripts path.

    EMPIRICAL confirmation of the static defects on this live install:
    * F5 CONFIRMED (with real consequence): `set 2 boot on` placed the EF00
      ESP-type GPT flag on PARTITION 2 (the ext4 /boot), not the real vfat ESP
      (sda1). parted: "2 ... ext4 boot  boot, esp". Firmware ESP-by-type lands on
      a partition with NO EFI payload -> the node boots via the Luna
      netboot->localboot chain (BootCurrent=EFI Network), not a clean disk ESP
      entry. So F5 is not cosmetic on this firmware path.
    * F3 CONFIRMED: ESP is FAT16 (blkid SEC_TYPE="msdos") from `mkfs.fat -F 16`.
    * F8 REVISED: EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI + fbx64.efi ARE present (from the OS
      image's shim RPM, NOT created by diskfull -- correcting the earlier guess).
      No persistent "Shim1" disk NVRAM entry stuck (BootOrder has a dangling
      0000); boot relies on the netboot->localboot chain (matches the VMware
      NVRAM flakiness noted for the new installer in JOURNAL §G 2026-06-04).
    * F4 (parted `name 1"` typo) ran harmlessly (no set -e); partition 1 just
      came up unnamed/efi-labelled -- consistent with static call.
    Note: diskfull is single-disk (sda); sdb/sdc/sdd still carry stale md
      superblocks from the prior install (inactive md124-127) -- diskfull never
      touches them (expected; the F6 re-entrancy concern is raid1-specific).
2b. raid1 dd-failover (Task #21) — STALLED: node001 did NOT come back after the
    reboot into the raid1 attempt. Symptoms (2026-06-07): ping 100% loss / SSH
    "No route to host"; ARP for node001 INCOMPLETE; NO new DHCP request; NO
    /boot/install/node001 fetch in the daemon log for this cycle. => the node
    never PXE'd for raid1 — it hung at FIRMWARE on the reboot, most likely due to
    the prior diskfull install leaving VMware EFI unbootable: F5 put the EF00/ESP
    GPT flag on the wrong partition (ext4 /boot, not the vfat ESP) AND a dangling
    Boot0000 NVRAM entry sat first in BootOrder. So this is a downstream
    consequence of the diskfull F5/NVRAM defects, not a raid1-install failure (the
    raid1 install never started). node001 IPMI is dead; recovery needs a vSphere
    power-cycle (govc is installed but unconfigured here). Node pre-set back to
    install_mode=auto/default so a power-cycle yields a clean reinstall.
    AWAITING operator power-cycle to resume. The raid1 + dd-failover (confirm F1)
    is deferred to the next session/cycle once node001 is recovered.
    [ ] 2c. tmpfs-by-hand diskless — pending (will work now F10 is fixed; the
            legacy template's shim-guard runs a -qpart .rd field directly).

